== assertion 7: every rule, on a fixture built to trip it
   the unreachable view is built by the cluster admin, not by alpha: measured,
   a LOGIN owner is reachable by ownership alone and a NOLOGIN one is
   reachable by membership, and the rule was right about both.
   policy_exists_rls_disabled -> policies_without_rls
   rls_disabled_in_public -> reachable_no_rls
   rls_policy_always_true -> always_true
   rls_references_user_metadata -> admin_only
   rls_references_user_metadata -> direct_policy
   rls_references_user_metadata -> helper_routed
   multiple_permissive_policies -> always_true
   login_role_security_definer_executable -> escalate
   auth_rls_initplan -> bare_call
   rls_policy_correlated_membership -> correlated_membership
   rls_enabled_no_policy -> silent
   security_definer_view -> definer_view
   security_definer_view -> nested_definer_view
   10 rules, 13 fixtures, every rule naming itself and the object it fired on.
   scoped to kit_advisor_fixture alone, so each one is proven specific and not
   satisfied by whichever other rule happened to return a row.
   helper_routed -> via_functions may_read_admin on the SAME table
   direct_policy -> via_functions '(empty, the control)' on the SAME table
   helper_opaque -> silent, and that is the rule's measured limit: the catalog
      does not carry a string body, so no rule reads one. See the
      "what this does not find" block in advisor.sql and REPORT-kit-advisor-pgproc-01.md.
   invoker_view -> silent, as required.
   plain_reads -> silent, as required.
   definer_no_grant -> silent, as required.
   over_invoker_view -> silent, as required.
   on_spelled_invoker -> silent, as required.
   over_on_spelled_invoker -> silent, as required.
   six views, six reasons, all silent: the rule reads whether the view is an
   invoker, whether anything it reaches has policies, and whether a caller can
   reach it -- rather than counting views.
   the last two are the same view with the option spelled `on` rather than
   `true`. Postgres stores reloptions verbatim and core's own fixture writes
   `on`, so a rule matching only one spelling would pass this suite while
   missing the fleet's idiom.
   kit_advisor_fixture dropped, asserted gone.

== assertion 8: the credential audit reports the same tables to every role
   as alpha (owner, "$user" schema does not exist):
     policy reads   : (token_digest = ( SELECT cafaye.current_credential_digest() AS current_credential_digest))
     the audit says : public.api_keys -> token_digest
   as cafaye (cluster admin, "$user" schema IS cafaye):
     policy reads   : (token_digest = ( SELECT current_credential_digest() AS current_credential_digest))
     the audit says : public.api_keys -> token_digest
   as alpha_app (non-owner LOGIN, owns nothing):
     the audit says : public.api_keys -> token_digest
   three roles, one answer — the audit no longer depends on who is reading.
   and it is not the empty answer: the admin role's answer is asserted
   NON-EMPTY, because three roles agreeing on nothing proves only that the
   audit has stopped working.
   control: the trap is LIVE on this cluster — one policy, two renderings, and
     as cafaye: (token_digest = ( SELECT current_credential_digest() AS current_credential_digest))     (qualified=0)
     as alpha: (token_digest = ( SELECT cafaye.current_credential_digest() AS current_credential_digest))   (qualified=1)
   and the audit above is identical for both anyway. That is the whole
   assertion: the function no longer reads the text that differs.

